  "answer": "<p>The best answer is <b>\"Implement a lifecycle hook in the Auto Scaling group that moves instances into a Terminating:Wait state when scaling in, allowing them to finish processing tasks before being shut down.\"</b> Lifecycle hooks give your instances time to complete extra steps when an instance launches or terminates. By adding a termination lifecycle hook, you can ensure an instance will not be terminated until it signals that it finished its current job, which will significantly decrease the amount of incomplete processing sent to the third-party service.</p><p>Why the other answers are incorrect:</p><ul><li><i>\"Configure application logic on the instances to enable termination protection while a job is being processed, turning it off when the job completes.\"</i> — Termination protection can help avoid accidental manual deletions but does not prevent Auto Scaling from terminating instances for scale-in events. This approach is thus ineffective in this scenario.</li><li><i>\"Launch a new Auto Scaling group with a fixed minimum and maximum size of 2, running web proxy software, and reroute HTTP traffic through these proxies at the VPC level.\"</i> — This does not address the problem at hand: jobs being cut off during scale-in events. Adding proxies would not stop active jobs from being interrupted by instance termination.</li><li><i>\"Increase both the minimum and maximum capacity of the Auto Scaling group and relax the scaling policies, making the group less responsive to changes in queue length.\"</i> — This method could reduce the likelihood of scale-ins but increases cost and does not directly solve the issue of active tasks on instances that are terminated.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> Lifecycle hooks are designed for scenarios where you need to complete custom actions or drain tasks before an instance is fully terminated. They're ideal for queue-based processing, as in this use case.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You maintain an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances that processes jobs from an SQS queue, and the group scales based on the queue length. The processing uses an external web service, whose operators are observing a high number of failed and duplicate requests from your system. Upon review, you see that instance terminations due to scale-in are interrupting tasks mid-process. What is an efficient, low-cost way to reduce incomplete operations caused by premature instance shutdown?</p>",
